---
// Made up example of a review. Copy: advisory_based_specs/02_Replacement_Copy.md §17.
// This is not a client project and reports no results. Keep the label at the
// top visible, and do not turn this into a case study.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Kicker from '../components/content/Kicker.astro';
import { MAIN_CTA } from '../lib/routes';

const title = 'Example of a Founder Decision Review | PainToBuild';
const description = 'Read a made up example of a product review. See how I compare possible explanations, suggest a small test and explain what the results could mean.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} ogImagePath="/open-graph/example-review.png">
  <article class="wrap section prose">
    <Kicker>Example review</Kicker>
    <h1>What a Founder Decision Review looks like</h1>
    <p class="made-up" role="note">
      <strong>This example is made up.</strong> It shows the kind of review I would write. It is not
      a client project, and no results have been observed.
    </p>

    <h2>The decision</h2>
    <p>
      A founder has made an app for sharing expenses. They want to decide whether to add features
      or improve how they explain the app to possible users.
    </p>

    <h2>What I am assuming for this example</h2>
    <p>
      The person organizing the expenses needs the app. They can share a link that lets everyone
      else view the breakdown without installing it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The founder has made demos but has not shown this benefit clearly. There is no reliable
      information about whether people return to the app or would pay for it.
    </p>

    <h2>Possible reasons for the problem</h2>
    <p>
      People may not understand what the app helps them do. The demos may be reaching people who
      do not organize shared expenses. Entering the information may take too much effort.
    </p>
    <p>It is also possible that a spreadsheet or message already does the job well enough.</p>

    <h2>What I would try first</h2>
    <p>
      I would ask a small number of willing organizers to use the current app for a real shared
      expense. I would explain the link sharing feature and watch where they need help or stop.
    </p>
    <p>
      This would help me decide whether the first problem to address is the explanation, the steps
      in the app or the benefit itself.
    </p>

    <h2>What I would look for</h2>
    <p>Can the organizer enter the expense and share it? Can the recipients view the breakdown? What help does anyone need?</p>
    <p>
      When another shared expense comes up, does the organizer choose the app again? If no new
      expense occurs, that does not tell me whether they would return.
    </p>

    <h2>How the result could change my advice</h2>
    <p>If people cannot finish the task, I would look into the step that blocks them.</p>
    <p>If they finish but prefer their old method, I would ask why the app is not worth using again.</p>
    <p>If they use it again without help, I would check whether more people have the same experience before making a broader claim.</p>

    <h2>The later review</h2>
    <p>
      After the test, I would compare what happened with the possible reasons listed above. I would
      then explain what I recommend checking or changing next.
    </p>
    <p>A thank you or a demo view would not show that someone had adopted the app.</p>

    <div class="actions end">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href={MAIN_CTA.href}
         data-event="advisory_cta_click" data-event-page="/example-review/" data-event-cta-id="example-end">
        {MAIN_CTA.label}
      </a>
      <a href="/advisory/#founder-decision-review">See what the review includes</a>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .section { padding-block: var(--s7) var(--s8); }
  .made-up {
    background: var(--pain-tint);
    border-left: 4px solid var(--pain);
    border-radius: var(--r-card);
    padding: var(--s4) var(--s5);
    margin: var(--s5) 0 var(--s6) !important;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .end { margin-top: var(--s7); padding-top: var(--s5); border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline); }
  .end a:not(.btn) { font-weight: 600; }
</style>
